The cause was the instructions' own example: "you know" was listed as a filler word to leave out, so the model cut it from lyrics like "you know how I feel". The example is replaced rather than the filler rule dropped, since leaving out um/uh still makes subtitles easier to read. Detecting target-language lines in code and skipping them was rejected: telling languages apart on short lines like "OK" or "Linger" is unreliable, and the model already sees the context. Copying unchanged also keeps Whisper's misspellings ("Blosom"); accepted, since the old behaviour fixed those only alongside cutting and moving words. No unit test: a test on the prompt's wording would not catch the model changing lines; the check was translating all 52 English lines of the 2 h Japanese film with both prompts (18 changed before, 0 after).
